ue] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287698 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287699 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287700 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287701 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287702 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287703 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287704 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287705 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287706 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287707 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287708 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287709 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287886 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287887 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287884 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287885 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287888 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287889 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051908 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287306 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055075 1071 "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A \\cdot B$ for $A = {1, 10, 100}$ and $B = {a, b}$?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055075 1071 "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={1,10,100}$ and $B={a,b}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055075 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/aa/aac2b461bef7d5e1a7ae6e4c7b8b50ebeff86f14b45fc3f929a56ebc870e63c3.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd425d5211861a8c1b7944bd787bba5272a4640a61eb39ff63a477741c16fc93.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051920 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287436 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055077 1071 "If $A = {0, 1, 2}$ and $B = {x, y}$, which of the following elements belong to $(A \\cdot B) \\cdot A$?\n\n1. $(1, y, 2)$\n2. $((1, y), 2)$\n3. $((y, 1), y)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055077 1071 "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055077 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/07/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051926 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287650 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055078 1071 "If $A = {-1, 1}$ and $B = {u, v}$, which of the following elements belong to $B \\cdot B \\cdot A$?\n\n1. $(v, v, 1)$\n2. $((v, v), 1)$\n3. $(1, v, u)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055078 1071 "If $A={-1,1}$ and $B={u,v},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,v,1)$\n- $((v,v),1)$\n- $(1,v,u)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055078 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051932 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287164 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055079 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055079 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$N, Z, Q, R, C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055079 1074 "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- First, we note that $\\sqrt{-1}=\\text{i}$ is a complex number. Therefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-1}\\in C.\n$$\n\n- Since the imaginary part of $\\sqrt{-1}$ is nonzero, it does not belong to $N,Z,Q,$ or $R.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-1}\\notin N,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin Z,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin Q,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051938 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287279 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055080 1071 "If $A = {3, 6, 12, 24, 48, …, 98304}$, then $\\mid A|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055080 1071 "If $A={3,6,12,24,48,…,98\\,304},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055080 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({2}^{n-1})=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{N} & =98\\,304 \\\\ {2}^{N} & =65\\,536 \\\\ N & ={log}_{2}⁡(65\\,536) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(65\\,536)}{{log}_{10}⁡(2)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051940 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287531 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055082 1071 "If $S = {1, {2, 3}, 4, {5, 6}}$, then $\\mid S|=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055082 1071 "If $S={1,{2,3},4,{5,6}}$, then $|S|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055082 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $S$ is the number of elements in $S.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $S:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,{2,3},\\,4,\\,{5,6}\n$$\n\nNote that ${2,3}$ and ${5,6}$ each count as single elements of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ has $4$ elements, the cardinality is $|S|=4.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051949 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287632 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055086 1071 "If $A = {-200,-193,-186, …, 997}$, then $\\mid A|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055086 1071 "If $A={-200,-193,-186,…,997},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055086 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-200,\\,-193,\\,-186,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =7 \\\\  & =-193-(-200) \\\\  & =-186-(-193) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-200+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-200+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7N-207 & =997 \\\\ 7N & =1\\,204 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,204}{7} \\\\ N & =172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $172$ elements, we must have $|A|=172.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051957 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287460 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055089 1071 "If $X = {{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, {1, 2, 3, 4}, ∅}$, then $\\mid X|=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055089 1071 "If $X={{1,2,3,4,5},{1,2,3,4},∅}$, then $|X|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055089 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X:$\n\n$$\n{1,2,3,4,5},\\,{1,2,3,4},\\,∅\n$$\n\nNote that ${1,2,3,4,5},$ ${1,2,3,4},$ and $∅$ each count as single elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|X|=3.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051958 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287234 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055090 1071 "If $T = {{}, x, y, {v, x, {w}}}$, then $\\mid T|=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055090 1071 "If $T={{},x,y,{v,x,{w}}},$ then $|T|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055090 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\n{},\\,x,\\,y,\\,{v,x,{w}},\n$$\n\nNote that ${v,x,{w}}$ and ${}$ count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=4.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051989 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287652 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055095 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n$A = {n^{2} + 2n: n ∈ N, n \\ge 2}$.\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A$?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186055095 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{n}^{2}+2n:n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186055095 1074 "The condition after the \"$:$\" symbol\n\n$$\nn\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nstates that $n$ can be any natural number that satisfies the inequality $n\\ge 2.$ In other words, \n\n$$\nn={2,3,4,5,…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={n}^{2}+2n,\\,n\\in {2,3,4,5,…}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={2}^{2}+2(2)=4+4=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={3}^{2}+2(3)=9+6=15 \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={4}^{2}+2(4)=16+8=24 \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={5}^{2}+2(5)=25+10=35 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA={8,15,24,35,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287890 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287913 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287925 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287915 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287963 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287964 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287966 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287967 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287965 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287822 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287823 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287824 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287825 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287832 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287833 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287834 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287835 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287836 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287837 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287838 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287839 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287991 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287992 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287987 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287995 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287988 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287994 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287997 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287998 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288000 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288001 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287826 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287827 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287830 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287831 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186059871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287878 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057319 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287578 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060159 1071 "Determine the entry $a_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060159 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060159 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057325 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287145 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060160 1071 "Determine the entry $a_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060160 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060160 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057510 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287429 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060180 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060180 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = \\frac{2}{x^{2}} - 3x^{3/4}$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060180 1074 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-3\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{(3/4-1)} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-3}-\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{-1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057528 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287622 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060183 1071 "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y = f(x)$. What is the global minimum of $y =-2f(x + 1)$?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060183 1071 "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060183 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057588 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287154 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060193 1071 "Let $a = ⟨ - 1, 3, 2⟩$ and $b = ⟨5, 1, 4⟩$. Find $3a + b$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060193 1071 "Let $a=⟨-1,3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨5,1,4⟩.$ Find $3a+b.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060193 1074 "To compute $3a+b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,3,2⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,9,6⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057630 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287515 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060200 1071 "Let $a = ⟨2x, 1, 4⟩$, $b = ⟨2, 0, 3⟩$ and $\\mid a \\cdot b|= \\sqrt{13}$. Find $x$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060200 1071 "Let $a=⟨2x,1,4⟩,$ $b=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{13}.$ Find $x.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060200 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2x & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(6x-8)j+(0-2)k \\\\  & =⟨3,-(6x-8),-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-(6x-8))^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36{x}^{2}-96x+64+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77} & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+77 & =13 \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+64 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057672 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287499 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060207 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of  linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+y=5 \\\\ x+y=6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060207 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n${-x + y = 5; x + y = 6$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060207 1074 "Notice that the system is already organized so that the variables are in the order $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient. \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057678 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287481 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060208 1071 "Given that\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes $a_{22} = 0$?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060208 1071 "Given that\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060208 1074 "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057696 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287533 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060211 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+7y=-5 \\\\  & 7x+11y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186060211 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${2x + 7y =-5; 7x + 11y =-4$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186060211 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \