 = a + λb$, find $λ$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097170 1071 "![](images/81/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097170 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/67/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094969 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287232 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097171 1071 "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c = λa + μb$, find $λ$ and $μ$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097171 1071 "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097171 1074 "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095047 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287437 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097184 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=3 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & {x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097184 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 3; x_{2} - x_{3} = \\frac{1}{2}; x_{3} = 2$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097184 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(2)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})-(2)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3+2-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2},2⟩.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095053 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287552 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097185 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097185 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097185 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=5 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(2)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)+4(2)=5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5-6-8=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=-9$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-9,-2,2⟩.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095065 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287422 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097187 1071 "![](images/3e/3e4a5e92ae49632cab61874a43c5afa8f308640e9ce70482ac2b6e610f97cae0.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c = λa + 3b$, find $λ$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097187 1071 "![](images/4b/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097187 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=\\frac{1}{2}a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095077 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287410 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097189 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y+6z=5 \\\\  & y-10z=-11 \\\\  & 4x+8y+13z=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097189 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x + 2y + 6z = 5; y - 10z =-11; 4x + 8y + 13z = 9$\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097189 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{11}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095089 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287292 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097191 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y+z=14 \\\\  & y+z=8 \\\\  & z=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097191 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n${x + 2y + z = 14; y + z = 8; z = 4$\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097191 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095095 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287247 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097192 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} - 2x$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186097192 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-2x.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186097192 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186100883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287933 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287898 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287899 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287900 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186100956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287959 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287687 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099013 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287489 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101287 1071 "The coefficient of $x^{7}y$ in the expansion of $(x + 2y)^{8}$ is\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101287 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ in the expansion of ${(x+2y)}^{8}$ is" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101287 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+2y)}^{8},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=2y,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+2y)}^{8}=(\\frac{8}{0}){x}^{8}(2y)^{0}+(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}+(\\frac{8}{2}){x}^{8-2}(2y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{8}{8}){x}^{8-8}(2y)^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}y$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1} & =8{x}^{7}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =16{x}^{7}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ is $16.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099043 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287294 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101292 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3 + x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101292 1071 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101292 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.2$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.2)^{5} & =(3+0.2)^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(0.2)+270(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =243+405(0.2)+270(0.0\\,4) \\\\  & =243+81+10.8 \\\\  & =334.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(3.2)^{5}-334.8|}{(3.2)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099085 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287651 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101299 1071 "${(x-2y)}^{6}=$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101299 1071 "What is $(x - 2y)^{6}$?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101299 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-2y)}^{6},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-2y,$ $n=6.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-2y)}^{6}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2y)^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0})(x)^{6}(-2y)^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1})(x)^{6-1}(-2y)^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2})(x)^{6-2}(-2y)^{2}+… \\\\  & =1(x)^{6}+6(x)^{5}(-2y)+15(x)^{4}(4{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{6}-12{x}^{5}y+60{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099109 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287516 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101303 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=5.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186101303 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{4x - y}$ that satisfies $y(0) = 5$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186101303 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4\\cdot 0}}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+C & ={e}^{5} \\\\ C & =\\frac{4{e}^{5}-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=5$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4}).\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287862 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287945 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287865 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287867 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287941 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287943 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287944 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287990 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287840 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287912 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287916 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287917 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287919 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287920 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287841 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [1759218610