17592186045585 false] [17592186106094 1071 "![](images/98/98b3193783f0ac19bcb78035b133ad8dbf956528dfb7474606cc586d3bfecc34.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $90{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106094 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & ≈139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102422 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287459 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106096 1071 "![](images/26/269b71c2ec5653b6577e904a43374a45a0830f671850c7157a8d1bbfb49308f3.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Given that $f^{′}(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f^{′}(x) < 0$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106096 1071 "![](images/26/269b71c2ec5653b6577e904a43374a45a0830f671850c7157a8d1bbfb49308f3.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106096 1074 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(0,2).$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102464 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287418 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106103 1071 "Find the angle between $a = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106103 1071 "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106103 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6+7\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{53}\\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{1961}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{1961}})≈{64.6}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102470 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287577 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106104 1071 "Let $p = ⟨\\sqrt{2}, 1,-1⟩$ and $q = ⟨\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{3}, 1⟩$. Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106104 1071 "Let $p=⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩$ and $q=⟨\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{3},1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106104 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}+1\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102512 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287343 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106111 1071 "Given that $a \\cdot b =-8$, find the angle between $a = ⟨2t, 2t⟩$ and $b = ⟨4,-2⟩$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106111 1071 "Given that $a\\cdot b=-8$, find the angle between $a=⟨2t,2t⟩$ and $b=⟨4,-2⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106111 1074 "First, since $a\\cdot b=-8$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-8 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 4+2t\\cdot (-2) & =-8 \\\\ 8t-4t & =-8 \\\\ 4t & =-8 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨2(-2),\\,2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{108.4}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102518 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287191 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106112 1071 "Find the angle between $a = ⟨3, 2, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 2, 3⟩$. Round your answer to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106112 1071 "Find the angle between $a=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,2,3⟩.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106112 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈{44}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102524 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287412 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106113 1071 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0, 4, 5)$, $Q(1, 5, 4)$, and $R(2, 3, 6)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106113 1071 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106113 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102530 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287466 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106114 1071 "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(3,-3, 1)$, $B(5,-3, 3)$, and $C(3,-2, 3)$. Round your answer to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106114 1071 "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(3,-3,1)$, $B(5,-3,3)$, and $C(3,-2,3).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106114 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨3,-3,1⟩,\\,b=⟨5,-3,3⟩,\\,c=⟨3,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,3⟩-⟨3,-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨3,-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{51}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102602 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287507 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106126 1071 "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $a = ⟨1, 3, 2⟩$ and $b = ⟨4, 1,-2⟩$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106126 1071 "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $a=⟨1,3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106126 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 2)i-(1\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-8i+10j-11k \\\\  & =⟨-8,10,-11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{10}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{285}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{285}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102620 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287276 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106129 1071 "Calculate the area of the $△PQR$, if $P(4, 7, 3)$, $Q(5, 6, 2)$, $R(3, 8, 3)$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106129 1071 "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR$, if $P(4,7,3)$, $Q(5,6,2)$, $R(3,8,3).$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106129 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(5i+6j+2k)-(4i+7j+3k) \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3i+8j+3k)-(4i+7j+3k) \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102626 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287557 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106130 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△ABC$, if $A(2, 1, 2)$, $B(1, 2, 1)$, $C(3, 2, 3)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106130 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(1,2,1)$, $C(3,2,3).$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106130 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102650 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287215 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106134 1071 "Find $b \\cdot (c \\cdot a)$, if $-a \\cdot (3b \\cdot (-2c)) = 24$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106134 1071 "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $-a\\cdot (3b\\times (-2c))=24.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106134 1074 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a\\cdot (3b\\times (-2c)) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =24 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102656 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287672 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106135 1071 "Find $c \\cdot (b \\cdot a)$, if $b \\cdot c = ⟨2, 3, 0⟩$ and $a = ⟨1,-2, 2⟩$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106135 1071 "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $a=⟨1,-2,2⟩.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106135 1074 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102662 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287663 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106136 1071 "Find $a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)$ given that $a = ⟨1, 0, 2⟩$ and $b \\cdot c = ⟨ - 2, 1, 8⟩$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186106136 1071 "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186106136 1074 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186110942 1068 #ed