17592186045585 true] [17592186180123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180123 1071 "Given that $n \\ge - 3$ is an integer, simplify $((n + 6)!)/((n + 5)(n + 3)!) =$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186180123 1071 "Given that $n\\ge -3$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+6)!}{(n+5)\\text{ }(n+3)!}=$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180123 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)(n+5)(n+4)(n+3)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+5)$ and $(n+3)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+6)!}{(n+5)\\text{ }(n+3)!} & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot (n+3)!}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}}{\\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+4) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+10n+24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178373 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186180124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287377 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180124 1071 "Given that $m \\ge 1$ is an integer, $((m!)^{2})/((m + 1)!(m - 1)!) =$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186180124 1071 "Given that $m\\ge 1$ is an integer, $\\frac{(m!)^{2}}{(m+1)!\\,(m-1)!}=$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186180124 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+1)! & =(m+1)\\cdot m! \\\\ m! & =m\\cdot (m-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $m!$ and $(m-1)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(m!)^{2}}{(m+1)!\\,(m-1)!} & =\\frac{m!\\cdot m!}{(m+1)!\\,(m-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{m!\\cdot m\\cdot (m-1)!}{(m+1)\\cdot m!\\cdot (m-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{m!}\\cdot m\\cdot \\cancel{(m-1)!}}{(m+1)\\cdot \\cancel{m!}\\cdot \\cancel{(m-1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{m}{m+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186192723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288022 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186192753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288002 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191212 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186193238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287602 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193238 1071 "Use the substitution $u = e^{x}$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{x}}{1 + 3e^{2x}}dx$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186193238 1071 "Use the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193238 1074 "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/f5/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191234 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186193243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287474 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193243 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}dx$." 13194139533407 17592186045585 false] [17592186193243 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186193243 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287677 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287678 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287679 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287749 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287750 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186198272 1038 #edb/ref 