}{15} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({3}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{N} & =7\\,971\\,615 \\\\ {3}^{N} & =4\\,782\\,969 \\\\ N & ={log}_{3}⁡(4\\,782\\,969) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,782\\,969)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287328 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1069 "q-181576" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1071 "If $A={2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1072 #uuid "0f3f92d9-7968-5a40-b27d-a5448f33107a" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287705 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{alig