}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {9}^{n-1}={9}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{N-1} & =3\\,486\\,784\\,401 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{9}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401)}{{log}_{10}⁡(9)} \\\\ N-1 & =10 \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287446 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1069 "q-181606" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n}:n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1072 #uuid "d20cd909-d841-561f-a924-223c927b1fe5" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287710 1074 "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n},\\,n\\in N={1,2,3,4,…}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{1}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(1)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{3}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{4}=\\frac{1}{2}(1)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur sequence alternates between $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $+\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287643 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1069 "q-181612" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $S={p,q,r,p,s,t}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1072 #uuid "78d7dd4f-ada8-52b7-82f6-b798e943f119" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287711 1074 "Two sets are equivalent if they contain the same elements. It doesn't matter if the elements are listed differently or if one set has duplicates and the other does not.\n\nTo determine which of the given sets is equivalent to $S,$ we start by listing the elements of $S,$ ignoring any duplicates:\n\n$$\np,\\,q,\\,r,\\,s,\\,t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only set equivalent to $S$ is ${r,s,t,p,q}$ since it contains the same elements as $S.$\n\nThe other sets are not equivalent to $S:$\n\n- ${p,q,r}$ is missing the elements $s$ and $t.$\n\n- ${{p},q,r,s,t}$ is missing the element $p.$ Note that ${p}\\ne p.$\n\n- ${p,{q},r,s}$ is missing the elements $q$ and $t.$ Note that ${q}\\ne q.$\n\n- ${{p},{q},{r},s,t}$ is missing the elements $p,q$ and $r.$ Note that ${p}\\ne p,{q}\\ne q,$ and ${r}\\ne r.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287667 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1069 "q-181613" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $S={3,3,6,9,12,6,9}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1072 #uuid "cfeea06e-cb1f-5660-9bdd-c34e82806ddb" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287712 1074 "Two sets are equivalent if they contain the same elements. It doesn't matter if the elements are listed differently or if one set has duplicates and the other does not.\n\nTo determine which of the given sets is equivalent to $S,$ we start by listing the elements of $S,$ ignoring any duplicates:\n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,9,\\,12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only set equivalent to $S$ is ${6,12,9,3}$ since it contains the same elements as $S.$\n\nThe other sets are not equivalent to $S:$\n\n- ${{3,6,9},12}$ is missing the elements $3,6,$ and $9.$\n\n- ${3,6,9}$ is missing the element $12.$\n\n- ${3,6,{12}}$ is missing the elements $9$ and $12.$ Note that ${12}\\ne 12.$\n\n- ${1,3,6,9,12}$ has the element $1,$ which is not in $S.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287327 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1069 "q-181615" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $S={5,1,1,4,1,5}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1072 #uuid "d5883409-5a53-5eeb-80ac-193541365763" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287713 1074 "Two sets are equivalent if they contain the same elements. It doesn't matter if the elements are listed differently or if one set has duplicates and the other does not.\n\nTo determine which of the given sets is equivalent to $S,$ we start by listing the elements of $S,$ ignoring any duplicates:\n\n$$\n5,\\,1,\\,4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only set equivalent to $S$ is ${1,4,5}$ since it contains the same elements as $S.$\n\nThe other sets are not equivalent to $S:$\n\n- ${1,5}$ is missing the element $4.$\n\n- ${{5},{1},4}$ is missing the elements $5$ and $1.$ Note that ${5}\\ne 5$ and ${1}\\ne 1.$\n\n- ${1,…,5}$ means ${1,2,3,4,5}$ has the elements $2$ and $3,$ which are not in $S.$\n\n- ${{4},1,5}$ is missing the element $4.$ Note that ${4}\\ne 4.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287281 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1069 "q-181638" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1072 #uuid "46816a09-6733-575c-a227-bde0b55ae7b4" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287714 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{-x},-{e}^{-x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})=3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{3x})=9{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})=27{e}^{3x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{3x},3{e}^{3x},9{e}^{3x},27{e}^{3x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x+1}}\n$$\n