e can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,-{csc}^{2}⁡x,2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x,…}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287259 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1069 "q-181666" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡n=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡q=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1072 #uuid "7fce9496-6699-56f5-83fc-903c92a8799e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287721 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There is the only value $m=0$ such that $m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there is the only value $m=0$ such that $m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\pi ,0,\\pi ,2\\pi ,…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287305 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1069 "q-181672" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡n=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡q=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1072 #uuid "039d29b3-e9bc-5440-9809-620a4a049dfc" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287722 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Q$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{9\\pi }{4},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287274 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287723 1069 "q-181674" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287723 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" 13194139533407 1759218604