x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287241 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287624 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1069 "q-181692" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1071 "![](images/70/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1072 #uuid "b2547003-ad79-57a3-97bf-c8a4091bfc89" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287734 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287196 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287559 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287568 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1069 "q-181694" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1071 "![](images/c6/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1072 #uuid "1cd9f8d0-368b-57f9-99c0-0235eafbffc0" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287735 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287558 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287595 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287619 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1069 "q-181696" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1071 "![](images/5c/5cd989164ddda8a542098548f401a055e085943e35d95a63fe47b132f723d8ce.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1072 #uuid "644c8b7b-2ed7-59bb-b325-c2361f722e5e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287736 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287358 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1069 "q-181702" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{3})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1072 #uuid "c54f5b27-abdd-5f0e-891e-7fb28f946241" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287737 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})=6x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)=6 \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6)=0 \\\\ {g}^{(5)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>4,$ we have ${g}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nB={{x}^{3},3{x}^{2},6x,6,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=5,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4 \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)=0 \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>2,$ we have ${h}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nC={4x,4,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=3,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287199 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287308 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287421 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1069 "q-181704" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n${z}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1072 #uuid "f1cb23c2-1fc6-50c0-a109-f29b4b4fe8eb" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287738 1074 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some cubic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2,$ ${z}_{2}=3-\\text{i},$ and ${z}_{3}=3+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2})(z-{z}_{3}) \\\\  & =(1)(z-2)(z-(3-\\text{i}))(z-(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-z(3+\\text{i})-z(3-\\text{i})+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-6z+10) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-6{z}^{2}+10z-2{z}^{2}+12z-20 \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20=0}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287353 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1069 "q-181705" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1071 "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB={{b}_{n}\\,:\\,{b}_{n+1}=3{b}_{n}\\,,\\,{b}_{1}=2,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $B?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1072 #uuid "f681fa69-efb2-5261-b1b8-5e0cacf8d4a4" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287739 1074 "The elements of $B$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${b}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n+1}=3{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=2,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {b}_{3} & =3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(6) \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {b}_{4} & =3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =3(18) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={2,6,18,54,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287384 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1069 "q-181707" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{a}_{n}\\,:\\,{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-5\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=0,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1072 #uuid "a8936fe9-9dc9-520d-b212-1a883cf78067" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287740 1074 "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-5,\\,{a}_{1}=0,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}-5 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}-5 \\\\  & =-5-5 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}-5 \\\\  & =-10-5 \\\\  & =-15 \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{4}-5 \\\\  & =-15-5 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA={0,-5,-10,-15,-20,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287374 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1069 "q-181710" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1071 "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB={{b}_{n}\\,:\\,{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+{b}_{n}\\,,\\,{b}_{1}=0,\\,{b}_{2}=3,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $B?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1072 #uuid "3b446035-df93-53ce-88f1-f410a869420d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287741 1074 "The elements of $B$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${b}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=0,\\,{b}_{2}=3,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is a Fibonacci sequence with ${b}_{1}=0$ and ${b}_{2}=3.$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =3 \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={b}_{3}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {b}_{5} & ={b}_{4}+{b}_{3} \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ {b}_{6} & ={b}_{5}+{b}_{4} \\\\  & =9+6 \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={0,3,3,6,9,15,…}={0,3,6,9,15,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287153 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1069 "q-181711" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{a}_{n}\\,:\\,{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n}\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1072 #uuid "a1f6d4da-db8f-504b-bf4a-fe102d4688d0" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287742 1074 "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+2{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4+2(2) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+2{a}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2(4) \\\\  & =16 \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{4}+2{a}_{3} \\\\  & =16+2(8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA={2,4,8,16,32,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287211 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1069 "q-181712" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1071 "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB={{b}_{n}\\,:\\,{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+{b}_{n}\\,,\\,{b}_{1}=3,\\,{b}_{2}=1,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $B?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1072 #uuid "1204972d-2d7b-5603-ae28-18104455800d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287743 1074 "The elements of $B$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${b}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=3,\\,{b}_{2}=1,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =3 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={b}_{3}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {b}_{5} & ={b}_{4}+{b}_{3} \\\\  & =5+4 \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={3,1,4,5,9,…}={1,3,4,5,9,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287347 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1069 "q-181718" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1071 "If $f(x)=⌊3x+2⌋+2,$ then $f(0.5)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1072 #uuid "eeaf05e8-e171-5c7f-8ef7-b7ae274cc685" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287744 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $⌊x⌋$ equals the greatest integer that is smaller than or equal to $x:$\n\n$$\n⌊x⌋=n,\\,\\text{where}\\,n\\le x<n+1,\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=0.5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.5) & =⌊3(0.5)+2⌋+2 \\\\  & =⌊1.5+2⌋+2 \\\\  & =⌊3.5⌋+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⌊3.5⌋=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.5) & =⌊3.5⌋+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287320 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1069 "q-181719" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|3n|<10}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|3q|<10}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|3x|<10}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1072 #uuid "4302dd15-6000-5073-b484-7b81fc84a4c7" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287745 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|<10 \\\\ 3|x|<10 \\\\ |x|<\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{10}{3}<x<\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\frac{10}{3},\\frac{10}{3}).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|<10$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|<10$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287658 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1069 "q-181721" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1072 #uuid "b4290422-629b-5741-89bd-a435f41aa909" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287746 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287336 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1069 "q-181733" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1072 #uuid "30977a65-8757-5cdf-8629-62e1df834a09" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287747 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4x|\\ge 8 \\\\ 4|x|\\ge 8 \\\\ |x|\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx\\le -2\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|4n|\\ge 8$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|4q|\\ge 8$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287512 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1069 "q-181740" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+1>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+1<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+1<0}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1072 #uuid "c6d13915-d6fa-5b7a-8103-f1358e92b8fa" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287748 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287510 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1069 "q-181755" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:4n+7=-9}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1072 #uuid "c72d17c7-bbba-5560-9ea5-79c36b94cdcb" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287749 1074 "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the equation $4n+7=-9.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our equation for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4n+7 & =-9 \\\\ 4n & =-16 \\\\ n & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $-4\\notin N,$ we conclude that the set $A$ contains no elements. Hence, $A=∅.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287639 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1069 "q-181759" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:3n-5\\le -4}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1072 #uuid "2ca265c7-53e0-565e-90a1-8e194daf47ec" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287750 1074 "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $3n-5\\le -4.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & \\le -4 \\\\ 3n & \\le 1 \\\\ n & \\le \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, no natural number satisfies the inequality above. So, we conclude that the set\n$A$ contains no elements. Hence, $A=∅.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287423 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1069 "q-181761" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:|x-3|=4}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1072 #uuid "a8031755-9ccd-5ede-8487-d58c710ca978" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287751 1074 "The elements of the given set are the real numbers that satisfy the equation $|x-3|=4.$ So, we first need to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-3| & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-1,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=7,$ and both of these solutions are real numbers.\n\nTherefore, we can write the given set as\n\n$$\nA={-1,7}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287338 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1069 "q-181764" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={q\\in Q:|2q|=6}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1072 #uuid "748afdcc-0ffd-5a9e-a3b6-aa12b2d317b9" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287752 1074 "The elements of the given set are the rational numbers that satisfy the equation $|2q|=6.$ So, we first need to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2q| & =6 \\\\ 2q & =\\pm 6 \\\\ q & =\\pm \\frac{6}{2} \\\\ q & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=3,$ and both of these solutions are rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, we can write the given set as\n\n$$\nA={-3,3}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287208 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1069 "q-181767" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:|\\frac{n}{4}|=3}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1072 #uuid "a3d15cb1-a715-57c1-8c4c-6fa34faa4424" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287753 1074 "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the equation $|\\frac{n}{4}|=3.$ So, we first need to solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{n}{4}| & =3 \\\\ \\frac{n}{4} & =\\pm 3 \\\\ n & =\\pm 12 \\\\ n & =-12,12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $n=-12$ and $n=12.$ However, since $-12\\notin N,$ we conclude that the set $A$ contains only one element, namely $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given set as\n\n$$\nA={12}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287268 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1069 "q-181768" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-2n\\le 0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-2q\\le 0}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1072 #uuid "29b1f7f7-72db-5315-ae82-b2adaab04321" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287754 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/49/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287179 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1069 "q-181770" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1072 #uuid "5558c8b0-025f-54bf-b54a-ad58d686884f" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287755 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287654 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1069 "q-181773" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:|n+6|<1}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1072 #uuid "34f3304b-4db8-56be-9ff5-f7a8b624854b" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287756 1074 "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $|n+6|\\le 1.$ So, we first need to solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n+6|<1 \\\\ -1<n+6<1 \\\\ -7<n<-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality does not have natural solutions.\n\nTherefore, the set $A$ is a null set:\n\n$$\nA=∅.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287441 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1069 "q-181775" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1072 #uuid "1e98098c-88d7-594b-ad3c-687cf15456dc" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287757 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times R\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07a3d9a6c38b5bfcc529496735a1a66eaceefd5ae3437a2cb0cd45911eccafae.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line.\n\n![](images/e4/e454f1cf125486ca25acf15e26efd251a4a03b08e899814ab3e77db7898713c4.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nThis is the whole plane, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287151 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1069 "q-181777" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1072 #uuid "a66eead9-d6a1-5d8c-a676-bee7fbbe7fc5" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287758 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287235 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1069 "q-181779" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1072 #uuid "4fc5e64b-6205-5324-9efb-c4a7ebedcc24" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287759 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/06/067b3b386273b09d5614f934f6525bd3e43ff46f420eb8aa7e744f635ff6d101.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a870f65350ad00d258471079a34bec49a47d331c176dc7a91b3c7aae5ae968dd.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287532 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1069 "q-181782" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={k\\in Z:|4k-2|\\le 6}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1072 #uuid "edc26ecc-821e-590e-b96f-cd295dc0cb41" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287760 1074 "The elements of the given set are the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $|4k-2|\\le 6.$ So, we first need to solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4k-2|\\le 6 \\\\ -6\\le 4k-2\\le 6 \\\\ -4\\le 4k\\le 8 \\\\ -1\\le k\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integer solutions of the above inequality are $-1,0,1,2.$ \n\nTherefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA={0,\\pm 1,2}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287469 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1069 "q-181784" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=1}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1072 #uuid "a4dd772c-f833-5508-b899-e1d5dcd144d0" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287761 1074 "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}+2n) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=…,-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{9}{2},…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={…,-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{9}{2},…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287491 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1069 "q-181786" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=-1}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1072 #uuid "bfb15ac5-3cf7-5a3c-8850-bd0aa0e2ff9d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287762 1074 "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=-1\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287477 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1069 "q-181787" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=1}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1072 #uuid "a4a6b6d7-3391-54b8-95ef-c14a05aebd99" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287763 1074 "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=1\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi x & =2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287319 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1069 "q-181788" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=-1}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1072 #uuid "de45387f-871a-54f3-8872-681188adbf68" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287764 1074 "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=-1\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi x & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =1+2n \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287323 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1069 "q-181790" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:3{x}^{2}+27=0}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1072 #uuid "0c60f4a2-135e-5608-9f14-4bfdbb84d5b8" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287765 1074 "The elements of the given set are the real numbers that satisfy the equation $3{x}^{2}+27=0.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+27 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =-27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x\\in R$ and $\\pm 3\\text{i}\\notin R,$ we conclude that the set $A$ contains no elements. Hence, $A=∅.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287298 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287331 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287393 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287433 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287621 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1069 "q-181791" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1071 "![](images/af/af3a9e323fa460b60bfa900c7edbcceb4057c442803160b2b4b5bd0704b4db7c.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1072 #uuid "80918b0e-9572-56bb-b137-be14e694fe5d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287766 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in {-1,0,1,2},\\,y\\in {-2,-1,0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le 3}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287342 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1069 "q-181794" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:2{n}^{2}-30<2}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1072 #uuid "fa55a98f-b9e1-5ef6-b4cd-a13600cf5441" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287767 1074 "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2{n}^{2}-30<2.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{n}^{2}-30 & <2 \\\\ 2{n}^{2} & <32 \\\\ {n}^{2} & <16 \\\\ \\sqrt{{n}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |n| & <4 \\\\ -4<n & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe natural numbers that are solutions to the inequality above are $1,2,3.$ Therefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA={1,2,3}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287176 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1069 "q-181795" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x)=0}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1072 #uuid "2a03fd25-2cb1-5f17-b3bf-18cdaa1eef89" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287768 1074 "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x)=0\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\pi ,\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\pm \\pi ,\\,…}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287318 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1069 "q-181799" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in {R}^{-}:3{x}^{2}\\le 27}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1072 #uuid "ac95ab3c-65a1-58d4-9a1b-af23a1357a51" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287769 1074 "The elements of the given set are the negative real numbers that satisfy the inequality $3{x}^{2}\\le 27.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & \\le 27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\le 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & \\le 3 \\\\ -3\\le x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative real numbers that are solutions to the inequality above are $x\\in [-3,0).$ Therefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA=[-3,0).\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287224 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1069 "q-181803" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1071 "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in {R}_{0}^{+}:9{x}^{2}+4\\le 4}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1072 #uuid "7826d097-e800-5831-a711-0d2ed26ca96e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287770 1074 "The elements of the given set are the positive real numbers and zero that satisfy the inequality $9{x}^{2}+4\\le 4.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+4 & \\le 4 \\\\ 9{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution to the inequality above that is in ${R}_{0}^{+}$ is $x=0.$ Therefore, $A={0}.$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287182 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287345 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287434 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287471 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1069 "q-181804" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1071 "![](images/a8/a87b00f9f7908fe62542ef7bca94277f040af97b4af24698dd648c167bcd3376.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,5,\\,2\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,5}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1072 #uuid "49eb2820-ef81-5aa5-ac35-475ea920738e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287771 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\,2<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the bottom edge ($y=2$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\,2\\le y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<5,\\,2\\le y<5}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287265 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287547 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287560 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287630 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1069 "q-181806" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1071 "![](images/a6/a659e2d14a645036488833cd920e6bb3e3d7a7716724dba63814be6df4066fe9.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,-1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1072 #uuid "c447c7d2-83cd-503b-b04b-0c9b2bcb0661" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287772 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287203 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287396 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1069 "q-181807" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1071 "![](images/8c/8c23fe5aa17b46293ff29cd8621ee10b2e143cb23b77811ddf20037a1639bce0.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1072 #uuid "3fc98003-bd40-5665-b2e9-cfd241ea5aff" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287773 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the left* of the curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx<{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x<{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287244 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287339 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1069 "q-181808" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1071 "![](images/66/664c70223ca8a6219b04a287c779d20bf0f88d770920d1357303721574d90787.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1072 #uuid "f80556dc-02e0-555b-8ef8-6bae1cda35bf" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287774 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287148 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287221 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287432 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1069 "q-181809" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1071 "![](images/8b/8be62d80165b756aedf31e998d6636d7f77a5606e195b3ec288cb0ac6fb52d6f.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1072 #uuid "706b2b43-7315-5cd0-9807-cc687006ade4" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287775 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,4).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+16(4)^{2}\\,>\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $>$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287363 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1069 "q-181941" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1071 "If $A={a,b}$ and $B={1,2},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(a,a,1)$\n- $(b,a,2)$\n- $((a,b),2)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1072 #uuid "e5ba42f9-2582-56fd-9cc1-974b79efc1dd" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287776 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${a,b}$ with ${a,b}$ and ${1,2},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (a,a,1),(a,a,2),(a,b,1),(a,b,2), \\\\  & (b,a,1),(b,a,2),(b,b,1),(b,b,2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(a,a,1)$ and $(b,a,2)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287611 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1069 "q-181942" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1071 "If $A={8,9}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(9,9,y)$\n- $(8,x,y)$\n- $(9,y,8)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1072 #uuid "5d67a16e-2bb4-53de-83ef-8253a6e9b7e6" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287777 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${8,9}$ with ${x,y}$ and ${8,9},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times A={ & (8,x,8),(8,x,9),(8,y,8),(8,y,9), \\\\  & (9,x,8),(9,x,9),(9,y,8),(9,y,9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(9,y,8)\\in A\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287464 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1069 "q-181945" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1071 "If $A={v,w}$ and $B={3,4,5},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,4,5)$\n- $(v,3,w)$\n- $(w,5,w)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1072 #uuid "3dced42a-f072-5a03-9e98-5ecc099c95d0" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287778 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${v,w}$ with ${3,4,5}$ and ${v,w},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times A={ & (v,3,v),(v,3,w),(v,4,v),(v,4,w),(v,5,v),(v,5,w), \\\\  & (w,3,v),(w,3,w),(w,4,v),(w,4,w),(w,5,v),(w,5,w)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(v,3,w)$ and $(w,5,w)$ are in $A\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287521 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1069 "q-181947" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1071 "If $A={p,q}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(p,q,2)$\n- $((p,p),4)$\n- $(q,q,6)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1072 #uuid "68270c3d-bf20-5dbc-8e60-99dca2522513" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287779 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${p,q}$ with ${p,q}$ and ${2,4,6},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (p,p,2),(p,p,4),(p,p,6),(p,q,2),(p,q,4),(p,q,6), \\\\  & (q,p,2),(q,p,4),(q,p,6),(q,q,2),(q,q,4),(q,q,6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(p,q,2)$ and $(q,q,6)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287381 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1069 "q-181957" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1071 "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={-1,1}$ and $B={x,y,z}?$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1072 #uuid "944fcff7-335e-5bcb-a482-39b5c66163af" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287780 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/8f/8fe661f4daa7a5bb75dea993f46bbf8970400a798e23da4018d3e4a0b891273d.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49270545404b21e8068b35cdb59c756e1f3bc3c22608359c8d7e83389e38c7a4.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287322 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1069 "q-181960" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=[0,2]\\times {-1,0,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1072 #uuid "dd0c147c-869f-5ac6-b075-b382d08ceaa2" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287781 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=[0,2]\\times {-1,0,1}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is either $-1,0,$ or $1.$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2],\\,y\\in {-1,0,1}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de9bd6dc79cabf8230b0cec26233958aa0fbb001875b9942cbb7f4d36e6b08fb.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $0.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/81/819c31e4d29dbd3e18c7dea8df495a565d2ede866ec019c764704d6dc47f5c70.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{3}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f335df98dc9cb33ccc96f87d24efa3bd78a4d4c271d0fbaf7ae5e8c30916d9e.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1a9065f9dbb6648100bc6a22bff367916c241dc2eba5f3878d6cf1b9821d4e8.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287443 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1069 "q-181963" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1071 "![](images/cb/cb3e6ef6a832f2adbbc5e096bddf2c4f6a0445fbd77a71df5617298f8dad2e51.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A={a,b,c}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(4,b)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1072 #uuid "acf6f0a0-0064-5b71-8b07-5e0f0eb96bda" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287782 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/57/57d053cca53046f02201540e655030a37ba8f7a2811e7dc724cf30ca9fd3c072.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(4,b)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287519 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1069 "q-181966" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1072 #uuid "8fc924c0-3fd1-5974-a50f-be993c31ee9b" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287783 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287538 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1069 "q-181969" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1071 "If $A={p,q,r}$ and $B={3,5},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times (A\\times A)?$\n\n- $(5,(r,p))$\n- $(q,(5,3))$\n- $(3,q,p)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1072 #uuid "1ec96a4e-9e05-5335-919b-b747e9d81df8" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287784 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${p,q,r}$ with ${p,q,r},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/aa/aad91ef7655fa8a5bf0c9852ee4e5e2166289087a56ac18e932ee3cce17164a0.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times A={(p,p),(p,q),(p,r),(q,p),(q,q),(q,r),(r,p),(r,q),(r,r)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |A|=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nB\\times (A\\times A)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $B$ and $A\\times A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/e5/e573cecaa9333e071d980b1b03d65dafadd027506d2c2b9b6e8acfbe15d3be52.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|B\\times (A\\times A)|=|B|\\cdot |A\\times A|=2\\cdot 9=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(5,(r,p))\\in B\\times (A\\times A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287569 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287592 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1069 "q-181970" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1071 "![](images/01/010d4befaa2bd2142cf63010f79f071df331bb6394eb65e4f883752cb04a2cb4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1072 #uuid "adce9191-abcc-50fc-a56f-a2f330169645" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287785 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -x+1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287333 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1069 "q-181975" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1071 "![](images/bf/bf1c37a92b184cb3e6c81bbd2d653008f5eff0e3b2131f2f297a336881661c22.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={p,q,r},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1072 #uuid "ffb21c72-0872-5ee1-9c14-274c6c7f743e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287786 1074 "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/91/919ad6e16dcd1d8f02e8c15c7bd1dfa15f67232c99706dfbc1239e1f9a0f99e5.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287326 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287655 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1069 "q-181976" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1071 "![](images/11/11ec43034f97e66840da012f3beaf2d933fb03cfbe69f510ca5b68f1fd861f83.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1072 #uuid "827675a7-728a-52ab-b17b-7e94ef593363" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287787 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x+1.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287498 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1069 "q-181977" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1071 "If $A={4,8}$ and $B={x,y,z},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times (A\\times B)?$\n\n- $(4,(8,z))$\n- $(8,(8,x))$\n- $(8,4,y)$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1072 #uuid "6e14f595-c123-5de8-8c3d-4afeb7095ae5" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287788 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${4,8}$ with ${x,y,z},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/72/72feee192b949dcce7b0f10f4e1d772c809bcf73de6c66f15c7de75a4a1ff672.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(4,x),(4,y),(4,z),(8,x),(8,y),(8,z)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times (A\\times B)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A$ and $A\\times B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ae41857d4aee82a2a4ad838c20bbe72b05a1505a5f4df3aa4a86fda0fff0612.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times (A\\times B)|=|A|\\cdot |A\\times B|=2\\cdot 6=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(4,(8,z))$ and $(8,(8,x))$ are in $A\\times \\text{\\(}A\\times B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287656 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1069 "q-181978" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1071 "![](images/d6/d6ee87bd861491394c6643679f62cb4b94436bf2777bd2436b3169e779736e2c.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={h,k},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1072 #uuid "16f7d99c-b5b7-5532-9552-dac57833067d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287789 1074 "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/50/507f8f6306c309d5e894cfb1dabfc2019f371911f7878b196e1f329f1b37dfa6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287275 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1069 "q-181979" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1072 #uuid "93d5eecd-9d38-5be6-abbe-17d9f9d1df54" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287790 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287534 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287670 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1069 "q-181988" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1071 "![](images/34/34f5bb40fdcd4f71967ef2b1cc15328cf8d4d02cdd54b19a406273fae6c88ad4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x-1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1072 #uuid "dfc3e0f0-9ab6-551c-8d47-c8fca0b2295d" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287791 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x-1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287192 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287447 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1069 "q-181999" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1071 "![](images/c7/c7f7b1dc9bf4da61b573345aaf92fe0d29ea00ef5558a9fca985d8af25fa0a03.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1072 #uuid "53ac5dfb-f75e-5ef2-a84c-e58da3b6698e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287792 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>2.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287576 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1069 "q-182001" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times [-1,0]$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1072 #uuid "8eb13b5c-5049-574a-9dd7-f6e99016a3c1" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287793 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times [-1,0]\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a integer number and whose $y$-coordinate belongs to $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in [-1,0]}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,0]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/63/63199d9f35c331339e97ad3b2094a520d1fb6a9892bd0beac83538c43b9e2748.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03a6a90b3eff572ceed341de55a0d5e625e338ca18f2ae8ee85eed3bb5278167.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times [-1,0]$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png)" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287403 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1069 "q-182009" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1071 "For the sets $A={k,m},$ $B={-1,3,5},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(-1,k)\\in C$\n- $(k,m)\\in C$\n- $(m,3)\\in C$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1072 #uuid "110cd0b0-c05f-5fa3-a734-2910b10ec096" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287794 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c660ff6d042bdf228387e5ad55651f02b136f738a2e4fa99c3ef3b3faef3e3c5.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(k,-1),(k,3),(k,5),(m,-1),(m,3),(m,5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287236 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1069 "q-182011" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1071 "For the sets $X={x,z},$ $Y={a,b},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(a,x)\\in Z$\n- $(z,a)\\in Z$\n- $(z,z)\\in Z$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1072 #uuid "4b374349-dba8-556c-833d-28419c5d38a1" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287795 1074 "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b73e7aeb017bd9f7c50f7d110c7933763834eb391ac4a3e97d25f4e587a8a3d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,a),(x,b),(z,a),(z,b)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287523 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287603 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1069 "q-182013" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1071 "![](images/77/7703b8c621b268a9fcbb412cd3a0617045145d475024dc91cecfa09a18fc8712.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-2}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1072 #uuid "49192157-aea0-5727-927f-dcfccbbb5607" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287796 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *right* the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-2\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $x=-2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -2}.\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287425 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1069 "q-182015" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1071 "For the sets $X={5,10},$ $Y={r,s,t},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(5,r)\\in Z$\n- $(r,t)\\in Z$\n- $(10,s)\\in Z$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1072 #uuid "a2efc8f2-dfe5-5b29-94dc-09dde2c27a4f" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287797 1074 "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1eb13d93fbefc4c7285edef537a9fb3e21339f7a3b37a179eeb150db8d726c1b.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(5,r),(5,s),(5,t),(10,r),(10,s),(10,t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287184 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287300 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1069 "q-182016" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1071 "![](images/f5/f54c771d1b7045bbaa301c9d595a45dc64fbdfc6b1c83c152fb6fc892373ea06.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1072 #uuid "0067ce51-137c-57cd-8c4c-7e69e7b8398e" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287798 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287337 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1069 "q-182018" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1071 "For the sets $X={f,g,h}$ and $Z=X\\times X,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,g)\\in Z$\n- $(f,g,h)\\in Z$\n- $(h,h)\\in Z$" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1072 #uuid "55b1acd6-5df7-5bc0-a3b3-0f00f1d5d19b" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287799 1074 "The Cartesian product $X\\times X$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $X.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${X}^{2}=X\\times X$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/17/1718bd6f3f2eaa8dd8fe581b0d938b3b5f1928d5e56a11a324f145e7a481fabe.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times X$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(f,f),(f,g),(f,h),(g,f),(g,g),(g,h),(h,f),(h,g),(h,h)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533407 17592186045585 true] [17592186287800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287555 